@bio:

Álvaro Torrente is Professor of Music History at the
Universidad Complutense de Madrid, Chair of the Instituto
Complutense de Ciencias Musicales (ICCMU). He holds a
degree in Musicology from the Universidad de Salamanca and
a PhD from the University of Cambridge; he has been a
Visiting Scholar at New York University (1998) and Yale
University (2009), and Associate at the Faculty of Arts and
Sciences, Harvard University (2025).

Torrente has received the AMS Stevenson Award (2008) and
the Deustche Musikeditionpreis (2013). His publications
include <i>La ópera en España e Hispanoamérica</i> (2001),
<i>Devotional Music in the Iberian World</i> (2007), <i>Historia de
la Música en España e Hispanoamérica: La música en el
siglo XVII</i> (2016), and <i>The Cambridge History of Music in
Spain</i> (forthcoming). He is Associate Editor of <i>The Operas
of Francesco Cavalli</i> (Bärenreiter Verlag), and his
editions of early operas have been revived at prestigious
venues such as the Royal Opera House, Bayerische
Staatsoper, Theater an der Wien, and Teatro Real.

Torrente was Principal Investigator of the ERC-funded
project DIDONE: <i>The Sources of Absolute Music</i> (2019–24),
and co–PI of the <i>DeepMusic</i> (2021–23), <i>LexiMus</i>
(2023–26) projects, funded by the Spanish Agencia Estatal
de Investigación, as well as <i>MadMusic</i> (2025–27) and
<i>ChordIA</i> (2026–28), both funded by the Madrid Regional
Governement.



@@END: BIO